    {
      "tipo": "explicacion",
      "titulo": "Ser or estar",
      "texto": "**Ser** is common for identity, origin, profession and usual traits. **Estar** is used for location and many states or conditions.\n\n- Soy de Madrid. / **Estoy** en Madrid.\n- Soy alta. / **Estoy** cansada (feminine) or **cansado** (masculine).\n- Es profesor. / **Está** ocupado.\n\n**En casa** is the usual phrase for 'at home', with no article. **En la casa** refers to a particular house. **Aquí** is here, **allí** is there.\n\nThe forms are: estoy, estás, está, estamos, estáis, están. The written accent distinguishes **está** (a form of estar) from **esta** ('this', feminine), which comes in a later lesson."
    },
